.TH TRACKER 1 "August 29, 2026" "tracker 1.1.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
tracker \- simple project tracker by MattFor

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tracker
.RI "<command> " "[arguments] [options]"
.br
.B tracker
.RI "<project> <command> " "[arguments] [options]"

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tracker
tracks projects by discovering Git repositories and storing project metadata
such as a status, a free form note and when the project was last touched.

Running
.B tracker
with no command lists the tracked projects.

Commands that act on a project accept the project either after the command or
before it, so
.B "tracker edit 12 status active"
and
.B "tracker 12 edit status active"
are the same.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR help ", " h ", " -h
Show help. With an argument, show only that section:
.B "tracker help edit"
or, equivalently,
.BR "tracker edit help" .
The argument may be a command or a topic
.RB ( usage ", " commands ", " "short forms" ", " "project selection" ,
.BR statuses ", " "list filters" ", " timestamps ", " "row format" ", " notes ,
.BR options ", " configuration ", " examples ).

.TP
.BR version ", " v ", " -v
Show the program version.

.TP
.BR list ", " l ", " ls
List tracked projects. Accepts a search word, a limit,
.BR all " (also " a ", " max " and " full ") for no limit and no"
.BR display.filter ", a"
.B regex
pattern, list filters and temporary setting overrides in
.IR setting = value
form. Status filters are written
.BI s: status
to keep only those and
.BI !s: status
to drop them, comma separated for several.

.TP
.BR check ", " c ", " cc ", " info
Show everything known about a project: its tracking data, where it lives, how
much space it uses, its Git branch, remote, last commit and working tree state,
the language and version read from its build files and its note.

.TP
.BR add ", " a
Add a project, or scan a directory and add the Git projects below it.

.TP
.BR remove ", " rm ", " r
Remove tracked projects. Nothing is deleted from disk. Removing more than one
project at a time asks for confirmation unless
.B --yes
is given.

.TP
.BR edit ", " e
Change the
.B status
or
.B note
of the selected projects. The field names may be shortened to
.BR s ", " st " for status and " n ", " nt " for note,"
and the field fuses onto the command, so
.B "tracker 12 es blocked"
is
.B "tracker 12 edit status blocked"
and
.B en
is
.BR "edit note" .

.TP
.BR note ", " n ", " sn
Set the note of the selected projects without naming the field, for example
.BR "tracker note 12 review sunday" " or " "tracker 12 sn review sunday" .

.TP
.BR status ", " st ", " ss
Set the status of the selected projects without naming the field, for example
.BR "tracker 12 st blocked" " or " "tracker 12 ss blocked" .
With no arguments it lists every status in use and how many projects hold it.
Combined with an
.BI s: status
selector it moves every project from one status to another, for example
.BR "tracker status s:blocked paused" .
Projects that already hold the new status are counted, not rewritten.

.TP
.BR todo ", " td ", " todos
Keep a list of things to do beside the projects, in a database of its own. A
todo is a name, a status and a note, selected the way a project is: by ID, TID,
range, name,
.B all
or
.BI s: status .
Takes
.BR list ", " add ", " remove ", " clear ", " edit ", " note ", " rename ", "
.BR status ", " done ", " reopen ", " check ", " stats " and " undo ,
for example
.BR "tracker todo add write the changelog" " or " "tracker td 3 done" .
The action may follow the todo, so
.B "tracker td 3 st blocked"
and
.B "tracker td status 3 blocked"
are the same. With no action at all the whole list is printed. The list has its
own IDs, its own numbering and its own undo. A note may point at a project with
.BI @ name ,
printed as the project, its ID and the row it last held, in the colour of its
status; one that names no project, or more than one, is left as written. Both
sides then know about each other:
.B "tracker check <project>"
lists the todos whose name or note is about it, and
.B "tracker td check <todo>"
lists the projects it names. See
.BR todos.database ", " todos.new_status ", " todos.done_status ", " todos.sort
and
.B todos.newest_first
in the settings.

.TP
.BR forget ", " ignore
Stop tracking the selected projects and keep future scans from finding them
again. The paths are written to
.B scan.exclude
in the settings file, which a running daemon picks up on its next scan. Takes
.BR list ", " clear " and " "clear <pattern>" .

.TP
.BR init ", " i ", " scan
Scan a directory and merge what it finds into the database. Existing entries
keep their ID, status and note. A project that was moved is recognised by its
checkout and keeps its entry rather than being reported as deleted and added
again, unless
.B scan.detect_moves
is off.

.TP
.BR path ", " p ", " where
Print the path of a project, for example
.BR "cd \(dq$(tracker path tracker)\(dq" .

.TP
.BR undo ", " u ", " revert
Take back the last change made to the database: an edit, an add, a scan, a
removal. One change is remembered, the last one. Looking at projects is not a
change, and neither is a scan by the daemon, so neither of them can lose an
undo. Undoing twice puts things back. The database as it was is kept beside it,
as
.IR data.pkl.undo .

.TP
.BR move ", " mv ", " relocate
Move the settings, the database or the todo list somewhere else and point
tracker at the new place, for example
.BR "tracker move todos ~/synchronised-folder/todos.pkl" .
With no arguments it prints where all three live. A destination that is a
directory, or ends with a slash, keeps the name the file has, and the
.I .undo
copy beside a database travels with it. A path typed with a tilde is stored as
typed, so the same settings file suits another machine. The database and the
todo list are pointed at through
.B database.file
and
.BR todos.database ;
the settings cannot name themselves, so
.I ~/.config/tracker/settings.toml
is made a link to wherever they went. A file already waiting at the destination
is kept rather than overwritten and simply used, which is what a second machine
wants when the folder is already syncing. A running daemon follows on its next
scan.

.TP
.BR stats ", " summary
Show totals, a breakdown by status, the most and least recently touched
projects and the one last used with tracker.

.TP
.B completion
Print a shell completion script for
.BR bash ", " zsh " or " fish ,
completing command names and tracked project names for both
.B tracker
and
.BR t .
.PP
.nf
eval "$(tracker completion bash)"
tracker completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/tracker.fish
.fi

.TP
.BR settings ", " s ", " config
View or change the configuration. Takes
.BR edit ", " path ", " get " and " set .

.TP
.BR daemon ", " d ", " bg
Control the background scanner. Takes
.BR start ", " stop ", " restart ", " status ", " log " and " run .

.SH SHORT FORMS
A command and one of its own actions may be written as a single word, so
.B tracker dst
is
.BR "tracker daemon status" .

.TP
.BR ds ", " dst ", " dk ", " dr ", " dl
daemon start, status, stop, restart, log
.TP
.BR sg ", " sset ", " se ", " sp
settings get, set, edit, path
.TP
.BR ss ", " sn
set status, set note
.TP
.BR fl ", " fc
forget list, forget clear
.TP
.BR es ", " en
edit status, edit note
.TP
.BR tda ", " tdl ", " tdd ", " tdo ", " tdr ", " tds ", " tdn ", " tdc
todo add, list, done, reopen, remove, status, note, check
.PP
Only a command and its own action fuse.
.BR -- .

.SH SELECTION
.TP
.B ID
Permanent global ID.
.TP
.B TID
Temporary ID from the current sorted view.
.TP
.BI i: n
Force the permanent ID.
.B id:
is accepted too.
.TP
.BI t: n
Force the temporary ID.
.B tid:
is accepted too.
.TP
.BI # n
Force the permanent ID. Shells treat a bare
.B #5
as a comment, so quote it or use
.B id:5
instead.
.TP
.BI @ n
Force the temporary ID.
.TP
.BI : n
Force the temporary ID, a shorthand for
.BI t: n .
.TP
.IB n - x
Inclusive range, in either direction.
.TP
.IB n + x
Project n and the next x projects.
.TP
.BI s: status
Every project holding that status, comma separated for several.
.B st:
and
.B status:
are accepted too, and a status matches exactly or by prefix.
.TP
.B all
Every tracked project, with
.B display.filter
and
.B display.list_limit
ignored so that nothing is hidden. Prefer this to
.BR * ,
which the shell expands to a directory listing before tracker sees it.
.TP
.B name
Exact or partial project name.
.TP
.B path
Exact or partial project path. A path that exists on disk is resolved first, so
tab completed and relative paths work.
.PP
A temporary ID is the row a project had the last time tracker printed one: a
listing, a search, a status lookup, the projects a scan added. Whatever is on
the screen is numbered from 1, so the numbering never has gaps and a project
hidden by
.B display.filter
still gets a number whenever something shows it. Only printing rows renumbers,
so a scan finishing in the background cannot move a project out from under a
number that was already typed. A project that has not been shown has no row and
prints as
.BR - .
The numbering is kept in
.IR ~/.local/state/tracker/view.json .
.PP
.B i:
and
.B t:
accept ranges as well, so
.B t:3-7
and
.B i:5+3
settle a number that is both an ID and a TID. Setting
.B projects.number_preference
to
.B id
or
.B tid
decides every bare number instead of reporting the clash.
.PP
.B remove
and
.B forget
do nothing at all unless every selector resolved.

.SH FILTERS
.TP
.BI +m: value
Include partial name or path matches.
.TP
.BI -m: value
Exclude partial name or path matches.
.TP
.BI +r: regex
Include names matching a regex.
.TP
.BI -r: regex
Exclude names matching a regex.
.TP
.BI +s: status
Include a status, comma separated for several.
.TP
.BI -s: status
Exclude a status, comma separated for several.
.PP
On the command line the leading
.B +
may be dropped and
.B !
used in place of
.BR - ,
and the long names
.BR match: ", " regex: " and " status:
work as well. A status matches exactly or by prefix, so
.B s:arch
finds
.B archived
too.
.PP
.B display.filter
applies the same filters to every listing, the bare
.B tracker
included, so
.B "filter = [\(dq-s:archived\(dq]"
hides archived projects everywhere.

.SH BARE WORDS
A word with no command in front of it is a project lookup: it shows everything
about the project when it matches one, and a row per project when it matches
several. A word that names no project at all is then tried as a status, so
.B "tracker stable"
lists every project holding that status, the same as
.BR "tracker s:stable" .
A project name always wins over a status of the same name, and the fallback
only ever shows projects; commands that change or remove them still need
.BI s: status
spelled out.

.SH AMBIGUOUS NAMES
A partial name that matches several projects is settled by
.BR projects.conflict_resolution_preference :
.TP
.B starts_with
Prefer a project whose name starts with what was typed.
.TP
.B first_match
Take the first one in the current order.
.TP
.B frecency
The project reached for most through tracker, ranked the way zoxide ranks
directories: a count of uses, multiplied by 4 within the hour, 2 within the day,
0.5 within the week and 0.25 beyond it. Counting uses needs
.BR projects.track_usage .
.TP
.B zoxide
The project visited most, read from the zoxide database with
.BR "zoxide query --list --score" ,
falling back to the frecency of tracker's own usage when zoxide is not installed
or knows none of the candidates.
.PP
.B frecency
and
.B zoxide
list the clash the way the other preferences do when they cannot tell the
candidates apart.

.SH STATUSES
A status is free text; tracker only cares about the ones you use.
.B "tracker status"
lists them with their counts,
.B display.status_colours
gives each one a colour and
.B sorting.status_order
sets the order
.B "sorting.by = \(dqstatus\(dq"
uses, with anything left out of it sorting after the rest.
.PP
.B projects.auto_status
gives a project its status from how long ago it was last touched, using the
ages listed under
.BR [projects.auto_status_rules] ,
where the entry set to
.B 0
catches everything older than the rest. Only those statuses and
.B projects.default_status
are ever rewritten, so a status set by hand is left alone.

.SH TIMESTAMPS
The date a project was last touched comes from the newest file inside it. The
directories in
.B projects.ignore
and the file patterns in
.B projects.ignore_files
are skipped while it is worked out, so a database, a log or a cache the project
writes for itself does not make it look freshly touched. Set
.B scan.timestamps_skip_ignored
to
.B false
to count everything.
.PP
Every project carries when it was last touched on disk
.RB ( last_touched )
and when it was last looked at with tracker
.RB ( last_used ).
.BR check ", " path " and " edit
and a bare project lookup all count as looking at one, unless
.B projects.track_usage
is off. Either may be sorted by and shown as a column.
.PP
.B display.relative_times
prints
.RB \(dq "2 days ago" \(dq
in place of the timestamp and
.B display.relative_style
chooses how it is written:
.B long
gives
.RB \(dq "8 seconds ago" \(dq
and
.B short
gives
.RB \(dq "8s ago" \(dq.

.SH ROW FORMAT
.B display.format
lays each row out by hand instead of using
.B display.columns
and the separators. The known fields are
.BR $tid ", " $id ", " $name ", " $path ", " $status ", " $last_touched ,
.BR $last_used ", " $version ", " $language " and " $note .

Fields are padded so the output stays aligned.
.B $>
aligns a field to the right and
.BI ${ name }
lets text follow a field immediately. Anything that is not a known field is
printed as written.

A format containing
.B $note
places the note itself, otherwise the usual inline or below placement is used.

Quote a format with single quotes; a shell expands
.B $name
inside double quotes.
.PP
.nf
tracker list 'display.format=$>tid. $name [$status]'
tracker settings set display.format '${tid}) $name - $note'
.fi

.SH NAMES
A name longer than
.B display.name_max_width
is shortened, breaking on
.BR - ", " _ ", " .
and on the capitals of a CamelCase name.
.B display.name_style
chooses how:
.B truncate
cuts the name at a boundary and ends it with
.BR display.name_continuator ,
so
.B von-neumann-machine-simulator
becomes
.BR von-neumann... ,
while
.B abbreviate
shortens every word instead, giving
.B von-neum-mac-sim
and
.BR VonNeuMacSim .
.B full
never shortens a name.

.SH NOTES
A note is printed on the same line as its project and only drops to a line of
its own when the terminal is too narrow. This is controlled by
.BR display.note_position ,
.B display.note_min_width
and
.BR display.show_notes .
.PP
A note may colour itself. A colour name in braces lasts until
.BR {/} ,
so
.B "tracker note 12 {red}broken{/} since friday"
prints the first word in red. The long names
.RB ( {red} ", " {yellow} ", " {gray} ", " {bold} ", " {dim} ", " {reset} ", ...)"
and the one letter shorthands
.RB ( {r} ", " {g} ", " {y} ", " {u} ", " {m} ", " {c} ", " {w} ", " {k} ", " {b} ", " {d} ", " {/} )
are both accepted; anything else in braces is printed as written and the
colours vanish when the output is not a terminal.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR verbose ", " vv ", " --verbose
Explain what is happening while it happens.
.TP
.BR --yes ", " -y ", " --force
Do not ask for confirmation.
.TP
.B --
Treat everything after it as text rather than options or commands, for a note
that starts with a dash or contains a command name.
.TP
.BR --no-colour ", " --colour
Force colour off or on.

.SH CONFIGURATION
Tracker loads
.I ~/.config/tracker/settings.toml
and falls back to the defaults shipped with the package.
.B "tracker settings edit"
and
.B "tracker settings set"
create that file from the shipped copy the first time they are used; nothing is
ever written inside the installation prefix.

Run from a source checkout it loads
.I my_settings.toml
and then
.I settings.toml
from the checkout instead, and keeps the database there too.

Any setting left out of your file falls back to the built in default, so a
partial configuration file is fine.
.PP
The todo list draws itself with
.BR [display] ,
except for what
.B [todos.display]
sets. A key left out of it is inherited from
.B [display]
and then from the defaults, and the inheritance runs one way only:
.B [display]
never picks anything up from
.BR [todos.display] .
The keys are the ones
.B [display]
has, as far as a todo has them, and its columns are
.BR tid ", " id ", " name ", " status ", " created ", " updated ", " done_at
and
.BR note ", "
with
.B last_touched
and
.B last_used
understood as
.B created
and
.BR updated .
A
.B [todos.display.status_colours]
table sits over
.B [display.status_colours]
one status at a time.
.PP
A todo still wearing
.B todos.new_status
prints without a status, and the column itself is left out when nothing in the
listing has one worth showing.

Settings can be overridden for a single command:
.PP
.nf
tracker list display.list_limit=20
tracker list sorting.by=name output.compact=true
.fi
.PP
A setting may be named in full or shortened, anywhere one is expected. In order,
tracker tries the full name
.RB ( display.list_limit ),
the bare name, which is unique for every setting
.RB ( list_limit ),
the start of it
.RB ( list_lim ),
the start of every word
.RB ( l_l )
and its initials
.RB ( ll ).
A section shortens the same way, so
.B d.ll
works too. A name that still fits more than one setting is reported with the
settings it could mean, so
.B nmw
lists
.B display.name_max_width
and
.BR display.note_min_width .
.PP
.nf
tracker list ll=5 rt=true
tracker settings set name_max 20
tracker settings get by crp
.fi

.SH DAEMON
The daemon periodically scans the directories configured under
.B [daemon]
in the settings file, adding new projects and archiving ones that disappeared.
It runs detached and writes to a log file.

It also re-reads the settings file whenever it changes, so
.B scan.exclude
and the watched paths take effect without a restart.

.TP
.B tracker daemon
Start the daemon.
.TP
.B tracker daemon stop
Stop the daemon this configuration started. A daemon belonging to another
database or state directory is never stopped silently: it is reported, with the
database it watches, and stopping it needs
.BR --yes .
.TP
.B tracker daemon status
Show whether it is running, where its log is and what it watches.
.TP
.B tracker daemon log
Show the end of the daemon log.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TRACKER_SETTINGS
Use a different settings file.
.TP
.B TRACKER_DATA
Use a different database file.
.TP
.B TRACKER_TODOS
Use a different todo list file.
.TP
.B TRACKER_VIEW
Use a different file for the numbering of the last listing.
.TP
.B TRACKER_TODO_VIEW
Use a different file for the numbering of the last todo listing. Without it the
todo numbering follows
.BR TRACKER_VIEW .
.TP
.B TRACKER_SHARE
Look for the shipped help text and default settings in this directory.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tracker
tracker list
tracker list tracker
tracker list 20
tracker list -m:archived
tracker list s:current,planned
tracker list all
tracker list display.name_max_width=16 display.name_style=abbreviate
tracker 12 es blocked
tracker :3 en review sunday
tracker list display.relative_style=short
tracker status s:blocked paused
tracker add ~/Programming/Projects active "work in progress"
tracker check tracker
tracker edit 3-7 status completed
tracker id:12 note review sunday
tracker tracker st current
tracker remove tracker
tracker init ~/Programming
tracker stats
tracker todo
tracker todo add write the changelog
tracker td 3 done
tracker td 3 st blocked
tracker td list s:todo
tracker settings set display.list_limit 30
tracker daemon status
.fi

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tracker/settings.toml
Your configuration.

.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tracker/data.pkl
Project database.

.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tracker/todos.pkl
Todo list.

.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tracker/view.json
The numbering of the last listing, what a temporary ID points at.

.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tracker/todo-view.json
The numbering of the last todo listing.

.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tracker/daemon.log
Daemon log.

.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tracker/daemon.pid
Daemon process ID.

